nodecode-egov-law is a rewrite of TakeshiTGAL's TakeshiTGAL/hermes-plugin-egov-law:

  https://github.com/TakeshiTGAL/hermes-plugin-egov-law
  commit 2e96dd28108250b374160077d6e1d9fa4dea55f1
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 TakeshiTGAL (LICENSE)

The original is three Python tools for Hermes Agent over the e-Gov Law API v2,
plus a skill that told the agent how to read what they return. The rewrite
keeps the same three tools, under the same names and with the same arguments
(egov_law_search, egov_law_article, egov_law_revisions), and speaks the same
API from Common Lisp through nlk:http: a law is resolved the way a person
names it (official title, e-Gov abbreviation, the common short names e-Gov
does not index, a law number or a law ID, with an ambiguous name listed as
candidates), an article is read by its e-Gov element name (Article_32_2,
Paragraph_2, the law's own 附則) and its XML is flattened to text, and the
amendment history is grouped into what is scheduled, what is in force now and
what came before, with the day-before/day check pair each row carries. It
also answers a Lisp call (egov-law:article "労基法" "32"), an operator's
/egov-law command, and (help :egov-law) with the same guidance the original's
skill gave: cite source.attribution and url, do not write a law from memory.

Left out, because the shape they fit is Hermes's own: the Automation
Blueprint jp-egov-law:weekly-amendments (a template a Hermes user turns into a
cron job) and Hermes's tool-search registration and per-platform tool
switches; Nodecode's own cron cell and /cells do that work here. The original's
edition-level details that only matter to a model reading a 4,000-character
article are simplified: the call that hunts an article moved by an amendment
to its old version, and the note that points at an 附則 article restating it,
are not carried, and the text search answers the laws and snippets without the
original's per-sentence position filtering. Everything else — the aliases, the
law-type ranking, the full-width paragraph numbering, the 8 MB response cap
and the one-host rule — is carried.
